From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Thu, 1 Oct 2026 10:10:50 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] Draw the part of a television in front of the camera when the
 rest is behind it

Recorded in game on 2026-10-01, walking along the 150 ft cinema screen with
its near end behind the camera: the picture became rays of colour bars
shooting across the sky from the middle of the view, and half of it was
missing. A corner behind the eye has no projection. The engine refuses it and
Camera::ProjectPoints writes it as zero, the centre of the view; the quad over
those corners was then refused by ScreenTessellation (CrossesCameraPlane) and
drawn the old way anyway, as one image quad folded through the centre.

Such a screen is now cut at a plane 0.2 m in front of the camera and only the
part in front is drawn, exactly (webui/ScreenClip.hpp):

  * The screen is flat, so its view depth is affine in texture space; the part
    in front is the unit square cut by one line, three to five corners.
  * Four points inside that part are projected by the engine like any corner.
    With their view depths they fix the plane's own map from texture space to
    the view: each row is affine in (u, v) (position times depth, and depth),
    so it is a least-squares fit, well conditioned however small or oblique
    the screen looks. Points that are not one flat screen seen through one
    camera (one refused by the engine) are refused: reason clip_unsolved, not
    drawn that tick rather than drawn wrong.
  * Which way round the picture goes is ScreenQuad::Orient's own rule, asked
    through the map where the screen is in front of the camera.
  * The overlay item carries the map instead of corners (Item::clipped); held
    samples blend maps between game ticks as they blend corners. The producer
    reports drawn_clipped; occlusion is traced to the middle of the part in
    front rather than to the centre, which may be behind the eye.
  * The depth pass draws the part in front as a fan of up to four triangles
    (Geometry gains a fourth register, twelve vertices a draw; a whole screen
    is the same two triangles as before) and computes every pixel's texture
    coordinate and depth from the map's inverse (ScreenDepth::BuildFromMap).
    Calibration samples behind the near plane are skipped.
  * Without the depth pass, PerspectiveImage::AddClipped cuts the screen to
    the near plane and the view and halves its texture space, quadrant by
    quadrant, until every cell is within the tolerance: a uniform grid cannot
    do it, since the curvature grows as the cube of nearness.

DrawSurfaceQuad and the new DrawSurfaceClipped share one body for the surface
predicate, the descriptor and its marks. ScreenMotion::Blend takes eight or
nine floats.

Tested: ScreenClipTests (new) against a ray-traced pinhole: the cut for one,
two and three corners behind the eye; the map from four projected points to a
millionth of a pixel and of the depth, and within 0.02 px through float32
arithmetic 2 km from the origin; the refusals; the depth pass from the map,
pixel by pixel through ScreenDepth.hlsli (uv within 0.003 texel, depth within
2e-6); the fan covering exactly the part in front; the orientation agreeing
with Orient and CornerForTexture in nine poses; the curvature bound; cut
points shared to the bit. PerspectiveImageTests: AddClipped on the cinema
walked along (worst texel 0.65 px), a set turned beside the camera and one
wholly in front, every pixel in front and in view covered, nothing drawn
behind the near plane or outside the view, nothing for a screen wholly behind.
SceneDepth.cpp, WorldOverlay.cpp and the changed parts of WebUiService.cpp and
MediaScreens.cpp were compiled against MinGW's Windows headers with GCC and
Clang; not yet in game.

